A||N||5||mbramble||HELP!!! '87 2.5L Water Pump||||5||
Z||000000||mbramble||09-17-2000||12:11 PM||||How do I remove the pulley so that I can get to the bolts that hold the water pump to the block???<P>There is no setscrew, bolts etc., holding the pulley on.  It thought maybe it was an allen screw into the end but looking down with a mirror it looks as if the inside is threaded.....<P>Help......!!!<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||09-17-2000||05:00 PM||||That one is 'press on, press off'. you need access to a hydraulic press to do the job.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mbramble||09-17-2000||06:08 PM||||Had to take back the original replacement I got from a DAPS because the pulley was too narrow.<P>Picked up a unit at AutoZone and they loaned me a bearing puller/presser.  Got it all replaced.  What fun!!!<P>Love what the Haynes manual says "remove the water pump".  Wow, what help!!!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Brian84SE||09-17-2000||07:31 PM||||I just changed my water pump yesterday.  Had to take old and new pump to a garage.  They had a special tool to remove the pulley and another to press it back on.  Charged me $10.<BR>Well worth it!||||reg||1
Z||000004||mbramble||09-17-2000||08:05 PM||||My problem was that I couldn't get the old one off without removing the pulley.  I couldn't access probably 4 of the 7 bolts because either the pulley was in the way, or there were some wires in the way that couldn't be moved because the pulley was in the way.<P>The tool I was loaned worked quite easily even in the cramped quarters available in that part of the engine compartment.  Just takes quite awhile because of the limited amount of turn you can apply to the wrench.  Putting the pulley back on seemed like it was going to take forever.<P>Turned out to be the belt anyway!!  I had soaped it up to see if I could stop the squealing but could not so figured it must be the water pump.  But, I'm glad I replaced the water pump.  I'm pretty sure it was original equipment since the car has been in the family since about 70K miles and I KNOW my kids never did any work on it!!  It is about to hit 140K.  It was a good $20 investment for a lifetime warrantied new pump.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||corsica91||09-17-2000||08:06 PM||||I replaced mine a couple of weeks ago.  That baffled me for a couple of hours, you have to pull of the water pump to block housing.  If you have a/c there is a couple bolts on the front of the a/c pump that you have to remove.  And then the 3 bolts that hold in the water pump to block housing.  It's a tight squeeze.  It was a big pain but good luck.  I had a leak and it ended up being that little spacer between the a/c pump mounting plate and the water pump.  So I just replaced the pump anyways.  Hope this helps, if you have any more questions email me, corsica91@home.com<P>Nathan Fant||||reg||1
